[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The Queen's Gambit Accepted comes down to one rule for Black: take the pawn on c4, don't try to keep it, and use the time White spends winning it back to hit the center. Against most White moves that means ...e5 at once; against 3.Nf3, it means ...c5. Each board below shows one idea with the few moves that carry it out.}
*

[Chapter "Take the pawn, then hit back with ...e5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "2.c4"]

{After 1.d4 d5 2.c4, taking on c4 keeps the c8 bishop's diagonal open; in the Queen's Gambit Declined (2...e6), the pawn on e6 shuts the bishop in. It isn't a real gambit: White wins the pawn back with e3 and Bxc4. Use that time to hit the center: play ...e5 at once against 3.e3, 3.Nc3 and 3.e4, almost nine games in ten.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 {[%cal Gc8h3]}
2... dxc4 {[%csl Yc4] Black takes the pawn and gives up the center for a moment.}
3. e3 {[%cal Gf1c4] Opens the f1 bishop's diagonal: Bxc4 comes next.} e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Hits d4 before White has the pawn back.}
4. Bxc4 {The pawn is back.} exd4 {Black takes on d4 at once.}
5. exd4 {[%csl Yd4] Material is level. White's d-pawn now has no pawns beside it: an isolated pawn (see Key ideas: Pawn structures). Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Against 3.Nf3: ...c5 instead"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "3.Nf3"]

{After 1.d4 d5 2.c4 dxc4 3.Nf3, one game in ten, the knight guards e5. 3...e5? 4.Nxe5 wins the pawn back at once (Stockfish: +1.3, clearly better for White). Hit d4 with the c-pawn instead, after ...Nf6 and ...e6.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 {[%cal Rf3e5]}
3... Nf6 {Develops and stops e4.}
4. e3 {[%cal Gf1c4] Bxc4 comes next.} e6 {[%cal Gf8c5] Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal.}
5. Bxc4 {White has the pawn back.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4, the main freeing move of the Accepted. Next come ...a6, ...Nc6, ...Be7 and castling. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Each board shows one plan with the few moves that carry it out. The first two come from the d5 chain (3.Nc3 e5 4.d5 and 3.e4 e5 4.d5, about a third of your games), where Black plays against White's king. Then come an early queen trade and the line 3.e4 e5 4.Nf3. The plans around White's isolated pawn are on the Pawn structures page.}
*

[Chapter "The d5 chain: take on e4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "5.e4"]

{After 3.Nc3 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.e4, or 3.e4 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.Nc3. White's pawns on d5 and e4 gain space, but they block White's own long diagonal: a queen on f3 can't reach the rook on a8, so ...b5 can hold the extra pawn on c4. Only the knight on c3 guards e4. If it takes on b5, take on e4, then check on b4 so that White's king can't castle.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. d5 Nf6 5. e4 {[%cal Gc3e4] [%csl Yd5,Ye4]}
5... b5! {[%cal Gb5c4] Keeps the extra pawn on c4.}
6. Nxb5 {The most common move. The knight wins a pawn back, but it no longer guards e4.} Nxe4 {Takes White's center pawn.}
7. Bxc4 {Most White players take back on c4. 7.Nc3 holds out a little better (Stockfish: −1.2, clearly better for Black).} Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check. Blocking with 8.Nc3?? loses a rook for a bishop (see Key ideas: Tricks and traps).}
8. Kf1 {White's king gives up castling.} O-O {Black's king is safe. White's is stuck on f1, and the h1 rook is shut in. Stockfish: −1.7, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Against 6.Bg5: ...Bc5 hits f2"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "6.Bg5"]

{After 5...b5 6.Bg5, White pins the knight on f6, the piece that would take on e4. Black's answer is ...Bc5, aimed at f2, the weak square beside White's king. If White's knight takes on b5, Black gives up the bishop on f2 and wins the piece back with checks.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. d5 Nf6 5. e4 b5! 6. Bg5 {[%cal Rg5f6,Gf8c5,Gc5f2]}
6... Bc5 {[%cal Gc5f2] Develops and aims at f2.}
7. Nxb5 {Takes the pawn.} Bxf2+! {Gives up the bishop for a pawn and pulls White's king out.}
8. Kxf2 Nxe4+ {[%cal Ge4g5] Check, and the knight hits the bishop on g5.}
9. Ke1 Nxg5 {Black has the piece back and is two pawns up for now.}
10. Qe2 {[%cal Ge2e4] White's best: the queen covers e4. White's king can no longer castle. Stockfish: −0.8, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Queens off: castle long"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "6.exd4"]

{After 3.e3 e5 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.Qxd4 Qxd4 6.exd4. With the queens off, Black's king is safe. Develop fast and castle long: the rook then comes to the d-file in one move, against White's lone d-pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 4. Bxc4 exd4 5. Qxd4 Qxd4 6. exd4 {[%cal Gd8d4]}
6... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4 at once.}
7. Nf3 {Guards d4.} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Hits the knight that guards d4.}
8. Be3 {Guards d4 again.} Bxf3 {Gives up the bishop to double White's f-pawns.}
9. gxf3 O-O-O {[%cal Gd8d4] The rook hits d4 at once. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Against 3.e4 e5 4.Nf3: trade the c4 bishop"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "6.O-O"]

{After 3.e4 e5 4.Nf3 exd4 5.Bxc4 Nc6 6.O-O. Black has an extra pawn on d4, but White is ahead in development, and the bishop on c4 is White's best attacking piece. Offer to trade it with ...Be6. Black gives the extra pawn back on b7, but gets quick development and the half-open b-file.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 e5 4. Nf3 exd4 5. Bxc4 Nc6 6. O-O {[%csl Yc4,Yd4]}
6... Be6 {[%cal Ge6c4] Offers the trade. Stockfish's choice.}
7. Bxe6 fxe6 {[%cal Ge6d5] The pawn on e6 guards d5, and the f-file is half-open for Black's rook.}
8. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3e6] Hits b7 and e6.} Qd7 {Guards e6 and lets b7 go.}
9. Qxb7 Rb8 {[%cal Gb8b7] Chases the queen and takes the half-open b-file. Material is level again. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{An isolated pawn has no pawns of its own color on the files beside it, so only pieces can guard it. White gets one on d4 after 3.e3 e5 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4, and after 3.Nc3 e5 4.e3: about one game in five. The first two boards show each side's plan with it, and the third shows the early queen trade after 4.dxe5. The d5 chain after 4.d5 is in Key ideas: Plans for both sides.}
*

[Chapter "The isolated pawn: Black presses on d4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "8.Nc3"]

{After 3.e3 e5 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4 Nf6 6.Nf3 Bd6 7.O-O O-O 8.Nc3. The pawn on d4 gives White space, but only pieces can guard it. Black aims pieces at it and pins the knight on f3 that guards it. The square in front of the pawn, d5, is a fine square for a Black knight: no White pawn can chase it away. Trade pieces when you can, since in an endgame the pawn is only a weakness.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 4. Bxc4 exd4 5. exd4 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bd6 7. O-O O-O 8. Nc3 {[%csl Yd4,Yd5]}
8... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4.}
9. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6] White pins Black's knight in turn.} h6 {Asks the bishop to decide.}
10. Bh4 Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3,Gc6d4] Pins the knight that guards d4.}
11. Re1 {[%cal Ge1e8] White's rook takes the open e-file. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "The isolated pawn: White's knight on e5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "8.O-O"]

{As White, after 3.e3 e5 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4 Nf6 6.Nf3 Bb4+ 7.Nc3 O-O 8.O-O. The pawn on d4 guards e5, a strong square for a White knight. White's plan is active pieces: h3 stops the pin ...Bg4, then the bishop and the knight come to f4 and e5. Keep pieces on the board: trades help Black.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 4. Bxc4 exd4 5. exd4 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 O-O 8. O-O {[%cal Gd4e5,Gf3e5] [%csl Ye5]}
8... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Black hits d4.}
9. h3 {Stops ...Bg4, so the knight on f3 can't be pinned.} h6 {Black stops Bg5 the same way.}
10. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4e5] The bishop eyes e5.} Bd6 {Black offers a trade of bishops.}
11. Ne5 {[%csl Ye5] The knight sits on e5, guarded by the pawn on d4. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "After 4.dxe5: the pawn on e5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "5.Nxd1"]

{After 3.Nc3 e5 4.dxe5 Qxd1+ 5.Nxd1. With the queens off, each side has a loose pawn: White's on e5, Black's on c4. Black's knight hits e5 at once, and a check on b4 can trade off a piece that could guard it. Keep an eye on c4 too: ...Be6 guards it.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. dxe5 Qxd1+ 5. Nxd1 {[%csl Ye5,Yc4]}
5... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6e5] Hits the pawn on e5.}
6. Nf3 {Guards it.} Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check.}
7. Bd2 {Stockfish's choice. Against 7.Nc3, the most common move, guard c4 with 7...Be6.} Bxd2+ {Trades off a bishop that could guard e5.}
8. Nxd2 Nxe5 {Black takes the pawn on e5 and is a pawn up for now, with the c4 pawn to look after. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Four tactical patterns from this course, each on its own board, starting just before the mistake. The first three are Black mistakes to avoid. The last is a White mistake to punish, and it comes up often.}
*

[Chapter "...Bb4?? and Qa4+"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "5.e4"]

{After 3.Nc3 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.e4, ...Bb4 looks natural: it develops and pins the knight. But White's queen can check from a4 and hit the bishop at the same time, and nothing guards b4. It is the most common move here at club level. The same trick works a move earlier: 4...Bb4?? 5.Qa4+ (Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. d5 Nf6 5. e4 {[%cal Rd1a4]}
5... Bb4?? {Develops and pins, but the bishop has no guard.}
6. Qa4+! {[%cal Ga4e8,Ga4b4] Check, and the bishop on b4 is hit.} c6 {Blocks the check.}
7. Qxb4 {White is a piece up. Stockfish: +5.8, winning for White. Play 5...b5! instead.} *

[Chapter "...b5 against 3.e3: the long diagonal"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "5.axb5"]

{After 3.e3 b5?! 4.a4 c6 5.axb5. Black tried to keep the c4 pawn, and now taking back on b5 opens the long diagonal from f3 to a8. Here nothing blocks it, unlike the d5 chain, where White's own pawns on d5 and e4 stand in the way. Against 3.e3, don't hold the pawn: play 3...e5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 b5?! 4. a4 c6?! 5. axb5 {[%cal Rf3a8]}
5... cxb5?? {Guards c4, but opens the diagonal to the rook on a8.}
6. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3a8] Hits the rook. Black has to give up a piece to save it.} Nc6 {Blocks with the knight.}
7. Qxc6+ Bd7 8. Qa6 {White is a piece up. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "...Bg4 before castling: Bxf7+"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Diagram "6.Nf3"]

{After 3.e3 e5 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4 Nf6 6.Nf3, only Black's king guards f7, and White's bishop on c4 aims at it. A bishop on g4 then falls to a fork. Castle first, then play ...Bg4. The same weakness shows after 3...Nf6 4.Bxc4 Nc6 5.Nf3 Bg4?: 6.Qb3! hits f7 and b7, and 6...e6 7.Qxb7 costs a pawn (Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 4. Bxc4 exd4 5. exd4 Nf6 6. Nf3 {[%cal Rc4f7] [%csl Rf7]}
6... Bg4? {Pins the knight, but f7 is weak.}
7. Bxf7+! {[%cal Gc4f7] Gives up the bishop for the f7 pawn and pulls the king out.} Kxf7
8. Ne5+ {[%cal Ge5g4] Check, and the knight hits the bishop on g4.} Kg8
9. Nxg4 {White is a pawn up, and Black's king can't castle. Stockfish: +2.4, winning for White. 6...Bd6 and castling was the move.} *

[Chapter "White's mistake: 8.Nc3??"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Diagram "7...Bb4"]

{After 5...b5 6.Nxb5 Nxe4 7.Bxc4 Bb4+. Blocking the check with the knight looks natural, and one White player in three plays it. But the knight on c3 is pinned, and Black can take it twice. Two more White mistakes against 5...b5, 6.a4? and 9.a3?, are in Trap: White's mistakes against 5...b5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. d5 Nf6 5. e4 b5! 6. Nxb5 Nxe4 7. Bxc4 Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1]}
8. Nc3?? {Blocks the check, but the knight is pinned to the king.} Nxc3 {[%cal Gc3d1] Hits the queen.}
9. bxc3 Bxc3+ {[%cal Gc3a1] Check, and the bishop hits the rook on a1.}
10. Bd2 Bxa1 11. Qxa1 {Black has won a rook and a pawn for a bishop. Stockfish: −5.6, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "3...Nf6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Furman Variation"]
[ECO "D27"]

{The Queen's Gambit Accepted (2...dxc4) comes up in about one Queen's Gambit game in six. 3.e3 opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop, and Bxc4 wins the pawn back at once. Against Black's quiet development, ...Nf6, ...e6 and ...c5, White castles and chooses between two simple plans: trade queens on d8 for a safe endgame with slightly more active pieces, or keep the queens and push e4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 {The Queen's Gambit Accepted. Black gives up the center for a moment to free the c8 bishop.}
3. e3 {[%cal Gf1c4] Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop. Bxc4 comes next, and the pawn is back.} Nf6 {The most common reply. 3...b5 tries to keep the pawn: see the trap chapter on 3...b5.

3...Be6, in about one game in twenty, guards c4 but blocks Black's e-pawn. Play 4.Nf3 Nf6 (this is the Winawer Defense) 5.Nc3: Ng5 can hit the bishop, and Bxc4 follows (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White). If Black holds the pawn with 4...b5?!, 5.a4 hits the chain, as in the trap chapter (Stockfish +1.9, clearly better for White).}
(3... e6 4. Bxc4 {Against 4...a6 or 4...c5, play 5.Nf3 and castle: ...Nf6 soon brings back the main line (Stockfish +0.2, equal). Against 4...Bb4+, block with 5.Nc3 (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). Against 4...c6 or 4...Nc6, play 5.Nf3, then Nc3, castle and push e4 (Stockfish +0.9 and +1.0).} Nf6 5. Nf3 {The same position as in the main line after 5.Nf3. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
4. Bxc4 {Black's other fourth moves:

4...Bf5, in about one game in six, leaves b7 without a guard. 5.Qb3 hits it, as against 3...Bf5 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White). After 5...e6, develop first with 6.Nc3: the greedy 6.Qxb7?! Be4! gives Black enough play (Stockfish 0.0, equal). After 6...b6 7.Nf3 you reach the last position of 3...Bf5. 5...Be6? fails to 6.Bxe6 fxe6 7.Qxb7 (Stockfish +4.6, winning for White).

4...g6 prepares ...Bg7. Play 5.Nf3 Bg7 6.O-O (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White). GM Igor Smirnov likes 6.Qa4+, which sets two traps: 6...Qd7? 7.Bxf7+! (Stockfish +3.2) and 6...Nbd7? 7.Bxf7+! (Stockfish +4.7). But after 6...c6 White has nothing special (Stockfish +0.2), so we castle.

Against 4...Nc6 and 4...Bg4, see the trap chapter on 5...Bg4.} e6 5. Nf3 {Black's other fifth moves:

5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 and castle (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). 6...Bxc3+?! 7.bxc3 gives White the two bishops, and e4 comes next (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White). 6...Ne4?! 7.O-O also helps White (Stockfish +1.7, clearly better for White).

5...a6 6.O-O: 6...c5 is the main line, and after 6...b5, play 7.Bd3 Bb7 8.a4 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).

5...Nc6 blocks Black's c-pawn, so ...c5 isn't possible for now, and White can push e4 more easily. Play 6.Nc3 (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).

5...Bd6: play 6.O-O and Nc3, and aim for e4 (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).} c5 {[%cal Rc5d4] Black hits d4 at once, the main freeing move of the Accepted.}
(5... Be7 {Slower. White uses the time to prepare e4.} 6. O-O O-O 7. Qe2 {[%cal Ge3e4] Prepares e4 and frees d1 for the rook.} a6 8. e4 {White has the big center.} b5 9. Bd3 {[%cal Ge4e5] Next e5 drives the knight away from f6, Black's best defender of the king. Stockfish: +0.6, slightly better for White.})
6. O-O {Castle first. Against 6...cxd4, take back with the pawn: 7.exd4 gives White an isolated pawn on d4 and active pieces (see Key ideas: Pawn structures).} a6 {[%cal Rb7b5] Prepares ...b5, which would gain time on the bishop.}
7. dxc5 {Takes the pawn before ...b5 can come with tempo.} Bxc5 {This is the Furman Variation.}
8. Qxd8+ {Trades queens. Black's king can't castle any more and stays in the center.} Kxd8 9. Be2 {The bishop steps back before ...b5 hits it.} Ke7 10. Nc3 b5 11. Nd2 {[%cal Gd2b3,Gb3c5] The knight heads for b3, where it hits the bishop on c5. White's pieces are a little more active. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Old Variation: 3...e5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{3.e3 comes up in about three games in ten. White prepares Bxc4 to win the pawn back. Hit the center first with 3...e5. After 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4, White has an isolated pawn on d4. Develop with ...Nf6 and ...Bd6 and castle, then bring the knight to c6 and pin with ...Bg4 to press on d4. Stockfish rates the main line as equal, and Black scores 54% after 8...Nc6 at this level.

3.Nc3 e5 4.e3 reaches the same position.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3
(3. Nc3 e5 4. e3 {About one White player in five plays 4.e3 here. The plan is the same as against 3.e3.} exd4 5. exd4 {Against 5.Qxd4, trade queens: 5...Qxd4 6.exd4 Nc6 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} Nf6 6. Bxc4 Bd6 7. Nf3 {Against 7.Bg5 or 7.Nge2, just castle: 7...O-O (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} O-O 8. O-O {The same position as in the main line after 8.Nc3. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
3... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Hits the center before White has won the pawn back. 3...b5?! tries to keep the pawn, and it fails: see Trap to avoid: 3...b5 and Qf3.}
4. Bxc4 {79% of White players.

Against 4.Nf3, take on d4: after 4...exd4 5.Bxc4, it is the same position as after 5.Nf3 in the note below. Against 4.d5, play 4...Nf6. After 5.Bxc4 or 5.Nc3, 5...c6! breaks up White's center (Stockfish −0.6 and −0.7, slightly better for Black).} exd4
5. exd4 {[%csl Yd4] 62% of White players. White's d-pawn is now isolated. Against 5.Qxd4, see Old Variation: 5.Qxd4.

Against 5.Nf3 (12%), check first: 5...Bb4+! 6.Bd2 Bxd2+ 7.Qxd2 Nc6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). 5...Bg4? runs into 6.Qb3, hitting b7 and f7 (Stockfish +1.5, clearly better for White). Against 5.Qb3, guard f7 with 5...Qe7. After 6.Nf3, the Christensen Gambit, 6...Qb4+ offers a queen trade (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nf6
6. Nf3 {47% of White players. Against 6.Nc3 (26%), see the next line. Against 6.Bg5, play 6...Be7 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 6.Ne2, 6...Bd6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).

Against 6.Qb3, which hits f7 and b7, play 6...Qe7+!: after 7.Ne2 Qb4+, the queens come off (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White). 6...Bd6? loses the f7 pawn to 7.Bxf7+ (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).}
(6. Nc3 Bd6 {[%cal Gd6h2] The same setup: ...Bd6 and castle.} 7. Nf3 {59% of White players. Against 7.Bg5 or 7.Nge2, just castle: 7...O-O (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} O-O 8. O-O {The same position as in the main line after 8.Nc3. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
6... Bd6 {[%cal Gd6h2] Stockfish's choice. The bishop eyes h2 and controls e5.

6...Bb4+ 7.Nc3 O-O 8.O-O is also played, but Stockfish rates it as slightly better for White (+0.5). Never 6...Bg4?: 7.Bxf7+! Kxf7 8.Ne5+ forks the king and the bishop (see Trap to avoid: ...Bg4? and Bxf7+).}
7. O-O {59% of White players. Against 7.Qe2+, block with 7...Qe7: after 8.Qxe7+ Bxe7, the queens are off and the game is level (Stockfish +0.2, equal). Against 7.Bg5, play 7...h6 8.Bh4 Qe7+ (Stockfish +0.2, equal). 7.Nc3 O-O 8.O-O reaches the main line.} O-O
8. Nc3 {39% of White players. 8.Bg5 (34%) h6 9.Bh4 Nc6 10.Nc3 Bg4 reaches the end of this line. Against 8.h3, which stops ...Bg4, play 8...h6 or 8...Nc6 (Stockfish +0.1 and +0.2, equal). Against 8.Re1, 8...Nc6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4.

Almost half the players at this level pin at once with 8...Bg4. That allows 9.h3! Bxf3 10.Qxf3, and White's pieces become active (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White). Bring the knight out first.}
9. Bg5 {52% of White players. Against 9.h3, which stops ...Bg4, play 9...h6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 9.d5, 9...Ne5 offers a knight trade (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} h6 {[%cal Gh6g5] Asks the bishop to decide.}
10. Bh4 {Against 10.Bxf6, take back with the queen: 10...Qxf6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). 10...gxf6? opens up your king (Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White).} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3,Gc6d4] Now the pin on the knight adds to the pressure on d4. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "3.Nc3: 4.d5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.d5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[ECO "D20"]

{The most important chapter. White's most common answer to ...e5 is 4.d5, and 3.e4 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.Nc3 reaches the same position: together about a third of your games. White's pawn on d5 takes space, but it blocks White's own long diagonal, and that makes 5...b5! possible. It keeps the extra pawn on c4, and if White's knight takes it, ...Nxe4 wins White's center pawn. After 5...b5, Black scores 67% at this level (Stockfish −1.0, clearly better for Black).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3
(3. e4 {This is the Saduleto Variation. Against 4.Nf3, see Central: 4.Nf3.} e5 {This is the McDonnell Defense.} 4. d5 Nf6 5. Nc3 {74% of White players. Now it is the same position as in the main line after 5.e4.

Against 5.Bg5, play 5...b5 or 5...Bc5 (Stockfish −0.8 and −1.1). Against 5.f3, 5...Bc5 (Stockfish −0.9). Against 5.Bxc4, take the center pawn: 5...Nxe4 (Stockfish −0.9, slightly better for Black).} b5 {[%cal Gb5c4] Keeps the extra pawn. The rest is in the main line. Stockfish: −1.0, clearly better for Black.})
3... e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Hits d4 at once. White chooses between 4.d5 (57%), 4.e3 (19%, see Old Variation: 3...e5), 4.dxe5 (13%, see 3.Nc3: 4.dxe5) and 4.Nf3 (7%).

Against 4.Nf3, take on d4. After 5.Nxd4, play 5...Nf6 (Stockfish −0.1, equal). After 5.Qxd4 Qxd4 6.Nxd4, play 6...Nf6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
4. d5 {[%csl Yd5] White closes the center and gains space. But the pawn on d5 blocks White's own long diagonal. Stockfish already rates the position as slightly better for Black (−0.7).} Nf6 {[%cal Gf6e4] Develops and eyes e4, so that the ...b5 and ...Nxe4 idea works later. Never 4...Bb4??: 5.Qa4+ wins the bishop (see Trap to avoid: ...Bb4?? and Qa4+).}
5. e4 {89% of White players. Against 5.Bg5, play 5...c6 or 5...h6 (Stockfish −0.5, slightly better for Black).} b5! {[%cal Gb5c4] Keeps the extra pawn on c4. Stockfish's first choice.

Why does ...b5 work here, when it fails after 3.e3? White's own pawns on d5 and e4 block the long diagonal, so Qf3 hits nothing. And a knight that takes on b5 leaves e4 without a guard.

5...c6 and 5...Bc5 are fine too (Stockfish −0.1 and +0.1, equal). 5...Bb4?? is the most common move here, and it loses the bishop to 6.Qa4+.}
6. Nxb5 {The most common move (39%); 6.Bg5 (34%) has its own line.

Against 6.a3 (9%) or 6.f3, play 6...Bc5 (Stockfish −2.2 and −2.6, winning for Black). Against 6.Nf3, 6...a6 (Stockfish −1.0, clearly better for Black). Against 6.Qc2, 6...c6 (Stockfish −1.1). 6.a4? loses material: see Trap: White's mistakes against 5...b5.}
(6. Bg5 Bc5 {[%cal Gc5f2] Aims at f2, the weak point next to White's king. Black scores 69% with it at this level.}
7. Nxb5 {35% of White players take the pawn. Against 7.Nf3, castle or play 7...Nbd7 (Stockfish −0.9 and −1.0). After 7.Bxf6 Qxf6, Black has the bishop pair and a lead in development (Stockfish −2.1, winning for Black).} Bxf2+! {Gives up the bishop for a pawn, to win the piece back at once.}
8. Kxf2 Nxe4+ {[%cal Ge4g5] Check, and the knight hits the bishop on g5.}
9. Ke1 {83% of White players. 9.Ke3? Qxg5+ (Stockfish −6.9) and 9.Ke2? Nxg5 (Stockfish −4.7) leave White's king even more exposed.} Nxg5 {Black has won the piece back and is two pawns up for the moment. White can no longer castle.}
10. Bxc4? {Takes back a pawn, but now the knight on g5 can always come back to e4. White's best is 10.Qe2, which covers e4. Then h4 traps the knight, and Black gets only pawns for it (Stockfish −1.0, clearly better for Black).} O-O {Black is a pawn up, and White's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: −3.3, winning for Black.})
6... Nxe4 {[%cal Gf6e4] Takes the center pawn: the knight on b5 no longer guards e4.}
7. Bxc4 {73% of White players. Stockfish prefers 7.Nc3 Nxc3 8.bxc3 Bd6 (Stockfish −1.2, clearly better for Black); after 7.Bxc4 it is −1.7. 7.Qa4 c6 is worse still (Stockfish −2.2, winning for Black).} Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check. White's best answer is 8.Kf1, which gives up castling. Against 8.Ke2, play 8...Bc5 (Stockfish −3.0, winning for Black). 8.Nc3?? loses a rook: see Trap: White's mistakes against 5...b5.}
8. Kf1 O-O {Black's king is safe, and White's king can no longer castle.

White's best is 9.Qe2: answer 9...Nf6 (Stockfish −1.6, clearly better for Black). 9.a3? walks into 9...Bc5!: see the trap chapter. Against 9.f3, hit the knight with 9...c6 (Stockfish −3.4, winning for Black). Against 9.Qf3, which hits the knight, play 9...Nd6 (Stockfish −2.5, winning for Black).}
9. Nf3?! {The second most common move, after 9.a3. It develops, but it lets Black win a pawn.} c6! {[%cal Gc6b5,Gc6d5] Hits the knight on b5 and the pawn on d5 at once.}
10. Nc3 {Half the White players retreat here. Against 10.Na3, play 10...Bb7 (Stockfish −3.6, winning for Black). 10.dxc6?? loses at once: 10...Qxd1+ 11.Ne1 Qxe1 is checkmate.} Bxc3 {Removes the knight that guards d5 and e4.}
11. bxc3 Nxc3 {[%cal Gc3d1] Black is a pawn up, and the knight hits the queen. Next comes ...cxd5. Stockfish: −3.1, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Normal Variation: 3.Nf3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Classical Defense, Rubinstein Variation"]
[ECO "D27"]

{Against 3.Nf3, one game in ten, ...e5 is impossible: the knight would take the pawn. Play the classical setup instead: ...Nf6, ...e6, ...c5 and ...a6, then ...Nc6, ...Be7 and castle. White wins the c4 pawn back, and the game is quiet and equal. Black scores 57% after 8...Be7 at this level.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 Nf6 {Develops and stops e4.}
4. e3 {40% of White players.

Against 4.Nc3 (40%), the Showalter Variation, play 4...a6!. After 5.a4, play 5...e6, then ...c5 and ...Nc6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). After 5.e4, 5...b5 6.e5 Nd5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 4.g3, 4...Nc6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 4.Qa4+, the Mannheim Variation, block with 4...Nc6 or 4...c6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} e6 {[%cal Gf8c5] Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal. ...c5 comes next.}
5. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits White's center: the main freeing move of the Accepted.}
6. O-O {74% of White players. Against 6.Nc3, play 6...a6, and after 7.O-O you reach the note on 7.Nc3 below.} a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5, which would gain time on the bishop.}
7. a4 {This is the Rubinstein Variation. A third of White players stop ...b5 at once.

Against 7.Nc3, play 7...b5 8.Bb3 Bb7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 7.dxc5, take back with the bishop: 7...Bxc5 is the Furman Variation, and after 8.Qxd8+ Kxd8 the game is level (Stockfish +0.2, equal). Against 7.Qe2, the Alekhine System, play 7...b5: the bishop has to move again, and ...Bb7 follows (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Develops and adds pressure on d4.}
8. Nc3 {66% of White players. Against 8.Qe2, take on d4: 8...cxd4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 8.dxc5, trade queens: 8...Qxd1 9.Rxd1 Bxc5 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Be7 {Castle next. After 9.dxc5, trade queens: 9...Qxd1 10.Rxd1 Bxc5. After 9.d5, 9...exd5 10.Nxd5 O-O. Against 9.Qe2, take on d4 or castle. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "3...e5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{Black hits back in the center at once. After 4.Bxc4 exd4 5.exd4, White has an isolated pawn on d4, but quick development and open lines for the pieces. Develop with Nf3 and Nc3, castle, and use the open e-file.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 {[%cal Re5d4] Black strikes at the center before White has won the pawn back.}
4. Bxc4 {Wins the pawn back first. The e5 pawn isn't going anywhere.} exd4
5. exd4 {[%csl Yd4] Now the d-pawn is isolated, but the c1 bishop's diagonal is open, and the e-file too. Against 5...Bb4+, block with 6.Nc3 and develop (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). Against 5...Nc6, play 6.Nf3 (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). 5...Bd6 6.Nf3 Nf6 reaches the position in the note at move 6.} Nf6
6. Nf3 {6...Bd6 is Black's best. After 7.Qe2+ Qe7 8.Qxe7+ Bxe7, the queens are off and the game is level (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} Bb4+ 7. Nc3 {Develops and blocks the check.} O-O
8. O-O {[%cal Gf1e1,Gc1g5] White is ready for h3, Bg5 or Bf4, and Re1. The pawn on d4 gives White space and the e5 square. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "3...Bf5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{About one Accepted player in eleven develops the bishop with 3...Bf5. White takes back on c4 and hits b7 with Qb3, as against the Slav. Most players then weaken their queenside with ...b6.

3...c5 is rarer. Take back with 4.Bxc4, and after 4...cxd4 5.exd4, develop with Nf3, Nc3 and castle: the isolated pawn gives White active play (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 Bf5
4. Bxc4 {4...Bxb1?! 5.Rxb1 gives White the bishop pair (Stockfish +1.7, clearly better for White).} e6
5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gc4e6] Hits b7, and the bishop on c4 eyes e6.} b6 {Six players in ten guard b7 this way. 5...Qc8 is the other main move.}
6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Nf3 {Castle next. White is ahead in development, and Black's queenside is loose. Stockfish: +0.8, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Central: 4.Nf3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Central Variation, McDonnell Defense"]
[ECO "D20"]

{Against 3.e4, play 3...e5 too. White's most common answer, 4.d5, is covered in 3.Nc3: 4.d5. About one White player in four plays 4.Nf3 instead. Take on d4, then develop with ...Nc6 and ...Be6, which offers to trade White's best attacking piece, the bishop on c4. After 8.Qb3 Qd7 9.Qxb7, Black has given the extra pawn back, but gets quick development and the half-open b-file. Stockfish rates it as slightly better for White (+0.3), and Black scores 46% after 5...Nc6 at this level.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e4 {This is the Saduleto Variation. White takes the whole center at once.} e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] This is the McDonnell Defense. Black hits back at once.}
4. Nf3 {26% of White players. Against 4.Bxc4, the Somov Gambit, take the pawn: 4...Qxd4! (Stockfish −0.7, slightly better for Black).} exd4
5. Bxc4 {51% of White players. Against 5.Qxd4, trade queens: 5...Qxd4 6.Nxd4 Bc5 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black). Against 5.Nxd4, play 5...Nf6 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Guards the extra pawn on d4.

Not 5...Bg4?: 6.Qb3 hits b7 and f7 (Stockfish +3.0, winning for White), and 6.Bxf7+ also wins material. Black scores 27% with it at this level.}
6. O-O {69% of White players. Against 6.Qb3, check first: 6...Bb4+ (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 6.Bb5, play 6...Bc5 (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black). 6.Ng5? is met by 6...Bb4+!, and White's knight on g5 is out of play (Stockfish −1.8, clearly better for Black).} Be6 {[%cal Ge6c4] Offers to trade White's strong bishop. Stockfish's choice.

6...Bg4? runs into 7.Qb3!, hitting b7 and f7 (Stockfish +2.3, winning for White). 6...Bc5 allows 7.Ng5 Nh6 8.Nxf7 Nxf7 9.Bxf7+ Kxf7 10.Qh5+ and 11.Qxc5. NM Solomon Ruddell calls 7.Ng5 decisive here, but Stockfish rates it as only slightly better for White (+0.5). 6...Be6 avoids it.}
7. Bxe6 {59% of White players. Against 7.Bb5, play 7...Bc5 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} fxe6 {[%cal Ge6d5] The pawn on e6 guards d5, and the f-file is half-open for Black's rook.}
8. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3e6] 68% of White players hit b7 and e6. Against 8.Ng5, guard e6 with 8...Qe7 or 8...Qd7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). 8.e5? gives Black's pieces the d5 square: play 8...Qd7 and castle long (Stockfish −1.5, clearly better for Black).} Qd7 {Guards e6. The b7 pawn is the price.}
9. Qxb7 {78% of White players take the pawn. Against 9.Ng5, castle long: 9...O-O-O (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Rb8 {[%cal Gb8b7] Chases the queen and takes the b-file.}
10. Qa6 Nf6 {Next ...Bd6 and castling. Material is level: White has taken b7, and Black still has the pawn on d4. White's pieces are a little more active, but Black's center and the half-open b- and f-files give enough play. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "3.Nc3: 4.dxe5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
