{After 3.Nc3 e5, about one White player in eight takes on e5. The queens come off at once, so Black's king is safe. Develop fast: ...Nc6 hits e5, and ...Bb4+ and ...Be6 bring the bishops out. Black scores 59% after 5.Nxd1 Nc6 at this level.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. dxe5 Qxd1+ {Trade queens.}
5. Nxd1 {72% of White players. Against 5.Kxd1, play 5...Be6: the bishop guards c4, and ...Nc6 and ...O-O-O come next, with check on the d-file (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6e5] Hits the pawn on e5.}
6. Nf3 {52% of White players guard e5 with the knight. Against 6.f4 or 6.Bf4, jump in with 6...Nb4!: the knight hits c2, and White's king and rook are in danger (Stockfish −1.1 against 6.f4 and −1.4 against 6.Bf4, clearly better for Black).} Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Stockfish's choice. The popular 6...Bg4 is fine too (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
7. Nc3 {59% of White players. Against 7.Bd2, trade and take the pawn: 7...Bxd2+ 8.Nxd2 Nxe5 (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Be6 {[%cal Ge6c4] Guards the extra pawn on c4. Black castles long next. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Old Variation: 5.Qxd4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{About one White player in six takes back on d4 with the queen. Trade queens at once. Without queens, Black's king is safe, and Black develops fast: ...Nc6, ...Bg4 and long castling put the rook on the d-file against White's d-pawn. Black scores 60% after 6...Nc6 at this level.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 4. Bxc4 exd4 5. Qxd4 Qxd4 {Trade queens. White can't keep them without losing time.}
6. exd4 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4 at once. 6...Nf6 and 6...Bd6 are just as good (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
7. Nf3 {52% of White players. Against 7.Be3, check with 7...Bb4+ (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 7.d5?!, the knight jumps forward: 7...Nb4 hits c2 and d5 (Stockfish −1.4, clearly better for Black). Against 7.Bb5, play 7...Be6 (Stockfish −0.6, slightly better for Black). Against 7.Ne2, 7...Nf6 (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Hits the knight that guards d4.}
8. Be3 {28% of White players. Against 8.Ne5, trade knights: 8...Nxe5 9.dxe5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). Against 8.Nbd2, castle long: 8...O-O-O (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 8.d5, take on f3 first: 8...Bxf3 9.dxc6 Bxc6 (Stockfish −0.8, slightly better for Black).} Bxf3 {Gives up the bishop to double White's pawns.}
9. gxf3 O-O-O {[%cal Gd8d4] The rook comes to the d-file at once and hits d4. Next ...Nf6, or ...Nge7 and ...Nf5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid: 3...b5 and Qf3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "3...e5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{Against 3.e3, almost one Black player in five tries to keep the pawn with 3...b5. It scores only 30% for Black at this level. 4.a4 attacks the pawn chain at its base, and the long diagonal from f3 to a8 opens. The natural 4...c6 5.axb5 cxb5 loses a piece to 6.Qf3. Don't hold the pawn: hit the center with 3...e5.

Compare 3.Nc3 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.e4 b5!, where ...b5 works: there White's own pawns on d5 and e4 block the long diagonal.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5
(3... b5?! {Holds the c4 pawn, for now.} 4. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits the base of the chain. The best defense, 4...b4, gives the pawn back after 5.Bxc4 (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White). 4...bxa4 5.Qxa4+ wins it back too (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} c6 {The most common move.}
(4... a6? 5. axb5 axb5?? {Opens the a-file.} 6. Rxa8 {The rook on a8 had no defender. Stockfish: +7.0, winning for White.})
5. axb5 cxb5?? {Holds c4, but opens the long diagonal to the rook on a8. 5...Nf6 was the best try, and White is still clearly better (Stockfish +1.9).} 6. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3a8] Hits the rook. Black has to give up a piece.} Nc6 7. Qxc6+ Bd7 8. Qa6 {White is a piece up. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.})
4. Bxc4 exd4 5. exd4 {Black has given the pawn back, but White's d-pawn is isolated, and Black develops freely (see Old Variation: 3...e5). Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 3...b5 and 4.a4"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "3...b5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{Almost one Accepted player in five tries to keep the pawn with 3...b5. It doesn't work. 4.a4 attacks the chain at its base, and the long diagonal from f3 to a8 becomes a weapon. Black's best is 4...b4, which gives the pawn back for a slightly worse game. The natural 4...c6 and 4...a6 lose material if Black takes back on b5 with the pawn. 4...Bd7, the second most common reply, fails too: after 5.axb5 Bxb5 6.Nc3 c6 7.b3!, White wins the pawn back with a big advantage, and the natural 7...cxb3? loses more.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 b5?! {Holds the pawn on c4, but the pawns on b5 and c4 can be attacked.}
4. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits b5, the base of the chain. 4...bxa4 5.Qxa4+ checks and wins the c4 pawn next (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White). 4...Ba6 5.axb5 Bxb5 reaches the 4...Bd7 line below.} c6?! {The natural way to hold b5, and the most common.}
(4... b4 {The best defense: Black gives the pawn back and keeps the queenside closed.} 5. Bxc4 {White has the pawn back and a free hand in the center. Stockfish: +0.8, slightly better for White.})
(4... a6? 5. axb5 axb5?? {Opens the a-file. 5...Bb7 was better, though White stays clearly better (Stockfish +1.0).} 6. Rxa8 {The rook on a8 had no defender. Stockfish: +7.0, winning for White.})
(4... Bd7?! {Guards b5 with the bishop. Almost one player in four chooses it.} 5. axb5 Bxb5 {The bishop on b5 now holds the c4 pawn.}
6. Nc3 {[%cal Gc3b5] Hits the bishop.

6...Ba6? loses a piece to 7.Qf3!: the queen hits the rook on a8, and the rook on a1 hits the bishop on a6 (Stockfish +7.8, winning for White).} c6 {Two players in three guard the bishop with the c-pawn.}
7. b3! {[%cal Gb3c4] Hits c4. Against 7...a5, 7...e6 or 7...e5, play 8.bxc4: the pawn is back and hits the bishop on b5. White has the bigger center and quicker development, and Black's queenside pawns are weak (Stockfish +2.3 after 7...a5, +3.0 after 7...e6).} cxb3? {The natural capture, chosen by two players in three. It opens the diagonal from f1 to b5.}
8. Nxb5! {[%cal Gf1b5] Takes the bishop. If Black takes back, the f1 bishop comes to b5 with check.} cxb5 9. Bxb5+ Nd7
10. Qxb3 {[%cal Gb5e8] White has both pawns back, and the knight on d7 is pinned. The a-pawn falls next: 10...a6 11.Bc6 Rc8 12.Rxa6. Stockfish: +6.0, winning for White.})
5. axb5 {Takes the base of the chain.} cxb5?? {Holds c4, but opens the long diagonal to the rook on a8. 5...Nf6 was the best try, and White is still clearly better (Stockfish +1.9).}
6. Qf3 {[%cal Gf3a8] Hits the rook on a8. Black has to give up a piece to save it.} Nc6 {The best try: the knight blocks the diagonal.}
7. Qxc6+ Bd7 8. Qa6 {[%cal Ga6b5] White is a piece up, and the b5 pawn is next. Stockfish: +5.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid: ...Bb4?? and Qa4+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.e4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[ECO "D20"]

{The most common mistake in this repertoire. In the d5 chain, ...Bb4 looks natural: it develops and pins the knight on c3. But White's queen can check on a4 and hit the bishop at the same time, and nothing guards the bishop. At this level, 5...Bb4 is the most common move in this position, and Black scores only 35% with it. Play 5...b5! instead. The same position comes from 3.e4 e5 4.d5 Nf6 5.Nc3.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. d5 Nf6
(4... Bb4?? {Pins the knight, but the queen check comes first. One Black player in six plays it.} 5. Qa4+ {[%cal Ga4e8,Ga4b4] Check, and the bishop on b4 is hit.} Nc6 6. dxc6 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 {White has won a piece. Stockfish: +6.5, winning for White.})
5. e4 b5! {Keeps the extra pawn, and there is no trick. Stockfish: −1.0, clearly better for Black.}
(5... Bb4?? {The most common move here: about one Black player in three plays it.} 6. Qa4+! {[%cal Ga4e8,Ga4b4] Check, and the bishop is hit. Half the White players find it.} c6 7. Qxb4 {White is a piece up. Stockfish: +6.0, winning for White.}) *

[Chapter "Trap: White's mistakes against 5...b5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Nxb5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted"]
[ECO "D20"]

{After 5...b5, three natural White moves lose material, and they come up often. After 6.Nxb5 Nxe4 7.Bxc4 Bb4+, one White player in three blocks with 8.Nc3??. After 8.Kf1 O-O, three in ten play 9.a3?. And one in sixteen plays 6.a4? at once. Black scores about 80% in each of these lines at this level.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nc3 e5 4. d5 Nf6 5. e4 b5
6. Nxb5
(6. a4? {Hits b5, but the knight on c3 has no good square.} b4! {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight.} 7. Na2? {The most common retreat. The knight is out of play on a2, and nothing guards e4 any more. 7.Nb5 is worse: 7...a6! traps the knight (Stockfish −6.1). After 7.Nce2, the best retreat, 7...Nxe4 still wins a pawn (Stockfish −3.0).} Nxe4 {Black is a pawn up, with much better pieces. Stockfish: −5.1, winning for Black.})
6... Nxe4 7. Bxc4 Bb4+
8. Nc3?? {Blocks the check, but the knight on c3 is pinned, and Black can take it twice.}
(8. Kf1 O-O 9. a3? {Hits the bishop, but it leaves f2 weak.} Bc5! {[%cal Gc5f2,Ge4f2] Two Black pieces hit f2. 10.Be3, the best defense, blocks the bishop.} 10. Be3 Bxe3 {[%engine ok] Takes the bishop and breaks up White's pawns. Stockfish's first choice in a deeper search; 10...Nxf2 also wins.} 11. fxe3 {[%cal Gc7c6,Ge4d2] Material is level, but White's king is stuck on f1 with no pawn left on f2, and White's kingside pieces haven't moved. Next 11...c6 hits the knight on b5, and if 12.dxc6, 12...Nd2+ forks the king and the bishop on c4. Stockfish: −5.6, winning for Black.})
8... Nxc3 {[%cal Gc3d1] Hits the queen.}
9. bxc3 Bxc3+ {[%cal Gc3a1] Check, and the rook on a1 is hit.}
10. Bd2 Bxa1 11. Qxa1 {Black has won a rook for a bishop, and a pawn, and White's king is still in the center. Stockfish: −6.1, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid: ...Bg4? and Bxf7+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6...Bd6"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{The same mistake turns up all over the Queen's Gambit Accepted. While Black's king is in the center, f7 is guarded only by the king. With White's bishop on c4, an early ...Bg4 lets White strike at f7 with Bxf7+, or hit f7 and b7 with Qb3. Castle first; ...Bg4 comes later (see Old Variation: 3...e5).

It happens in the 3.e4 lines too. After 4.Nf3 exd4 5.Bxc4, 5...Bg4? runs into 6.Qb3 (Stockfish +3.0, winning for White) or 6.Bxf7+, and after 5...Nc6 6.O-O, 6...Bg4? runs into 7.Qb3! (Stockfish +2.3, winning for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5
(3... Nf6 {The quieter move. The same trap waits here.} 4. Bxc4 Nc6 5. Nf3 Bg4? {Pins the knight, but b7 and f7 are now loose.} 6. Qb3! {[%cal Gb3b7,Gc4f7] Hits b7 and f7. 6...Bxf3?? 7.Bxf7+ Kd7 8.Qe6 is checkmate.} e6 7. Qxb7 {White is a pawn up. Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White.})
4. Bxc4 exd4 5. exd4 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bd6 {Develops and prepares to castle.}
(6... Bg4? {Pins the knight. One Black player in ten plays it here.} 7. Bxf7+! {[%cal Gc4f7] Gives up the bishop for the f7 pawn and pulls the king out.} Kxf7 8. Ne5+ {[%cal Ge5g4] A fork: check, and the bishop on g4 is hit.} Kg8 9. Nxg4 {White is a pawn up, and Black's king can't castle. Stockfish: +2.2, winning for White.})
7. O-O O-O {Now ...Nc6 and ...Bg4 are safe. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5...Bg4? and Qb3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...Bg4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Old Variation"]
[ECO "D20"]

{When Black's light-squared bishop leaves c8, b7 is loose. A queen on b3 then hits b7, and together with the bishop on c4 it hits f7 too. In this line, grabbing the knight on f3 even allows a mate in two.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 Nf6 4. Bxc4 Nc6 {Develops, but blocks the c-pawn, Black's main freeing break. 4...Bg4?! at once runs into the same 5.Qb3 (Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White).}
5. Nf3 Bg4? {Pins the knight, but b7 is now loose.}
6. Qb3! {[%cal Gb3b7,Gc4f7] Hits b7 and f7.} Bxf3?? {Takes a piece, but f7 falls with check.}
(6... e6 {Black gives up b7.} 7. Qxb7 {White is a pawn up, and the knight on c6 is attacked. Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White.})
(6... Na5? {Hits the queen and the bishop at once.} 7. Bxf7+! {The bishop escapes with check.} Kd7 8. Ne5+ {Another check, and the queen still has time to move.} Kc8 9. Qa4 {[%cal Ga4a5] The queen steps away and hits the knight on a5.} Bf5 10. Qxa5 {White is a knight and a pawn up, and Black's king is exposed. Stockfish: +7.8, winning for White.})
7. Bxf7+ Kd7 8. Qe6# *

